What this solution actually does: SWF_OUT_READ is the first stage of the outbound SWIFT chain (its work rows are consumed by SWF_OUT_ETL, which selects operations whose master row is in status READ). It is a timer-driven Windows Service (the host falls back to 10 seconds; the configured interval lives in the encrypted service configuration and is not visible here). Each tick opens a SimpleInjector scope, marks the service as running, loads the database business date and four system parameters (HORA_OP_INICIO, HORA_OP_CEP_FIN, the override flag SWF_OUT_GET_MT_BATCH, and SWF_CRITERIO_COMPLETO, which is required but never read), and decides whether to work: when SWF_OUT_GET_MT_BATCH is 1/true it always works and also includes the MT 998 batch; otherwise it requires a business day (the shared EsDiaHabil() is false on every Saturday and Sunday unless FORZA_EJECUCION_SERVICIOS=1) and a local time inside the window. When it works it (1) reads the pending outbound messages that T24 has staged in the view VW_T24_SWF_MESSAGES, through the shared ConexionT24Mw.LeerMensajesSwfT24() (stored procedure MUFG_MX2_ConsultaMensajesSwiftT24_MUFGLibreria in VistasT24); (2) in batch mode only, builds one proprietary MT 998 time-deposit position message per open deposit (:12:999, report id RI,OETD0) for every client listed in tb_bilun_mensajes_swift with mensaje = '998', using a 98-line query against the Insight data store (BS.BNK_LD_LOANS_AND_DEPOSITS, its LocalRef, and BS.BNK_CUSTOMER) that formats the SWIFT text in SQL; (3) stores every message with usp_SwfOutRead_InsertMensajesT24, which, when absent, creates a work-plan master row (status READ) and a work row in tb_srv_swf_trabajo keyed by folio and SWIFT_OUT (for MT-199 it also auto-authorizes the master row and queues an out-control row); and (4) asks T24 to flag every processed header (an OFS DE.O.HEADER ... STP.STATUS=MQ transaction queued through uSP_T24_ADD_TRX) and polls for up to 10 seconds for T24's confirmation. The destination BIC of each message is not carried by this service: the ETL resolves it from the routing table tb_bilun_mensajes_swift. The business meaning of the 998 report and the T24 behavior behind the OFS flag are inferred from code and SQL; the producer and consumer contracts are not in this repository.
