5. Architecture-Fit Note
The workload is a poll-based database-to-database bridge: a timer-driven Windows Service that, on every tick (the host's fallback is 10 seconds; the configured interval is not visible), reads the messages T24 has staged in its SWIFT view, stores them in TOMI work tables for the ETL, and asks T24 to flag them, plus a batch that generates MT 998 time-deposit reports from the Insight store. It is the first stage of a pipeline (read, ETL, MQ/package delivery) rather than a long-running saga, so the lowest-risk path is to modernize in place after correcting the findings, following the sequence in _ORCHESTRATION.md section 6: first remove the loss and false-signal risks (READ-FR-1, READ-FR-2, READ-FR-3, READ-FR-4, READ-FR-11, READ-FR-17) and the alert flood (READ-FR-5); then restore reproducible builds and meaningful tests (READ-FR-21, READ-FR-19); then simplify the design (READ-FR-9, READ-FR-14); and only then evaluate hosting changes. The staging tables plus the idempotent insert already give the "at-least-once with de-duplication" shape a reliable consumer needs for a single service instance (READ-FR-16); what is missing is per-message isolation, a truthful outcome, and a heartbeat for "no input". Two parts of the current shape are poor fits for a 10-second timer: the mark-in-T24-and-poll step, which is an awaited wait on another system (up to ten one-second delays plus query time per tick), and the MT 998 batch, which is naturally a scheduled job (once per data date) rather than a flag inside the loop (READ-FR-7). An event-driven hand-off (a queue or change notification from T24 staging) or a scheduled Worker Service would remove the polling, but those options depend on facts this audit does not have: message volume and peak rate, the latency SLA from T24 staging to the ETL, whether the CEP window is intended (READ-FR-6), the real timer interval, hosting constraints (the 32-bit shared library and D:\ServiceCMS deployment), and budget. A microservices rewrite is not justified by the current evidence.
